            <ArticleTitle>Gabapentin therapy for painful, blind glaucomatous eye: case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>377-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1526-4637.2006.00167.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Gabapentin is an antiepileptic used for neuropathic pain treatment. Glaucoma can be conceptualized an optic neuropathy associated with characteristic structural damage to the optic nerve and associated visual dysfunction.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To use gabapentin to relieve discomfort in a painful, blind glaucomatous eye of a patient unwilling to undergo further invasive treatment.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Patient rated his pregabapentin and postgabapentin pain by a verbal descriptor scale and a numerical scale.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Gabapentin provided significant pain relief (on a 0-10 scale, reduction from &quot;8&quot; to &quot;0-2&quot;) at regular visits over 6 months.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Gabapentin may relieve pain in a painful, blind, glaucomatous eye.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
